1
00:00:08,380 --> 00:00:10,340
Rym: It's Tuesday, April 2nd, 2024.

2
00:00:10,340 --> 00:00:10,721
Rym: I'm Rym.

3
00:00:13,636 --> 00:00:14,210
Scott: I'm Scott.

4
00:00:14,431 --> 00:00:16,090
Rym: And this is GeekNights Tonight.

5
00:00:16,590 --> 00:00:18,781
Rym: It's Slay the Spire versus Video Poker.

6
00:00:18,801 --> 00:00:20,469
Rym: We're talking about Balatro.

7
00:00:22,070 --> 00:00:23,230
Rym: Bal-spade-tro.

8
00:00:25,072 --> 00:00:25,803
Scott: Let's do this.

9
00:00:27,594 --> 00:00:29,929
Scott: Uh, it's been a while since we did a show.

10
00:00:30,390 --> 00:00:31,749
Scott: I went to PAX East without Rym.

11
00:00:32,509 --> 00:00:32,643
Rym: Yup.

12
00:00:32,971 --> 00:00:33,406
Scott: It was a PAX.

13
00:00:34,130 --> 00:00:40,609
Rym: First time you've been to a PAX that I was not at, but I've been to... You know, we don't count PAX devs, 'cause those are weird.

14
00:00:41,070 --> 00:00:42,256
Rym: I've been to three PAXes.

15
00:00:42,276 --> 00:00:44,829
Rym: you didn't go to, so my lead is narrowed to two.

16
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:45,795
Scott: Mm.

17
00:00:47,032 --> 00:00:59,169
Scott: Uh, it wasn't, you know... When you're not there with a lot of people, you can run out of things to do unless you really just want to play games with the few people you have.

18
00:01:00,852 --> 00:01:05,069
Scott: But, you know, when people are on different enforcing schedules, you gotta do panels, right?

19
00:01:06,511 --> 00:01:11,381
Rym: I feel like a lot of the default, like, you and I or you, me, and Emily often have some default things.

20
00:01:11,421 --> 00:01:15,670
Rym: we'll do in those gaps that if either one of us isn't there, that just disappears.

21
00:01:16,590 --> 00:01:16,680
Scott: Yup.

22
00:01:18,211 --> 00:01:21,661
Scott: You know, it was pretty crowded in the expo area, right?

23
00:01:21,861 --> 00:01:24,950
Scott: Total peoples was a lot, but not completely full.

24
00:01:26,012 --> 00:01:29,324
Scott: But once the expo hall closed, the tabletop area was quiet.

25
00:01:31,350 --> 00:01:41,170
Scott: It was better than last year, actually, which was, you know, but it's up versus recent years, but it's below the peak times, right?

26
00:01:41,251 --> 00:01:48,070
Scott: And I think, you know, part of this, PAX... It's weird, 'cause unplugged, you basically can't get a seat in tabletop anywhere, anytime.

27
00:01:48,891 --> 00:01:51,134
Rym: I think it's partly because there's a lot going on here.

28
00:01:51,154 --> 00:02:14,410
Rym: PAX East, despite being for a long time the biggest PAX, we definitely saw a trend where as soon as they started selling single day badges instead of just selling the four day badge or the three day badge, almost everyone who goes to PAX East, in my opinion, my experience, like my assumptions, I would love to see some data to prove me wrong.

29
00:02:14,931 --> 00:02:23,490
Rym: I think the majority of people who go to PAX East only go for a day, or even if they go for multiple days, they come in the expo hall and they fucking leave.

30
00:02:23,830 --> 00:02:29,869
Rym: And the people who stay for the entire PAX, or who stay after the expo hall closes, are the same people who always go to PAXs.

31
00:02:30,770 --> 00:02:36,469
Scott: Yeah, if we had a PAX of just the people who are like four day hardcore type people...

32
00:02:36,750 --> 00:02:38,670
Rym: Which, remember, all PAXs used to be that.

33
00:02:39,331 --> 00:02:44,008
Scott: Yeah, I think you could actually put it in a smaller building and it would be better.

34
00:02:45,130 --> 00:02:59,003
Rym: Yeah, and I think a big problem with PAX East is that part of why they went to the single days is that the four days would sell out right away, but some percentage of those people are the PAX lifers, and the other percentage of those people were the people who bought a four day badge.

35
00:02:59,023 --> 00:03:00,970
Rym: but they really only come for one day to see the expo hall.

36
00:03:02,011 --> 00:03:12,220
Scott: So if you look, it's like the cost of going to PAX East if you actually have to pay for the badge and the hotel is like a lot of money depending on how many people you share the hotel with.

37
00:03:13,250 --> 00:03:20,930
Rym: But unlike PAX West, which is in the middle of a vibrant city with a million restaurants right nearby, or PAX Unplugged, which it's just tabletop people.

38
00:03:22,551 --> 00:03:32,090
Scott: The next PAX East is going to be in May, so it won't matter as much that it's a little far away because you'll actually be able to walk the greater distance required comfortably.

39
00:03:32,830 --> 00:03:36,250
Rym: The thing is, the distance you gotta walk is all the way over that bridge into Chinatown.

40
00:03:37,290 --> 00:03:39,455
Scott: That's fine if it's warm out, right?

41
00:03:39,475 --> 00:03:40,738
Scott: That's not a distance at all.

42
00:03:40,798 --> 00:03:45,730
Scott: But in the cold, in the rain, like this PAX, it's like you don't even want to walk out to the food trucks.

43
00:03:47,011 --> 00:03:59,079
Rym: But it is just interesting because we've talked about this off and on for a long goddamn time, but there is definitely a big shift in the culture of all PAXes.

44
00:03:59,459 --> 00:04:04,343
Rym: when they shifted from you only buy weekend passes and the people who are there are there.

45
00:04:04,703 --> 00:04:14,790
Rym: for most of the parts of PAX to when it became there are expo hall people and then there's everybody else and very rarely do the two intersect except inside that expo hall.

46
00:04:17,111 --> 00:04:29,090
Scott: I think what PAX could use to enhance itself, right, because basically what's happened is the exhibitors, the industry, is going down, right?

47
00:04:29,731 --> 00:04:40,330
Scott: It's like, you know, they're laying people off, the games are cancelling, most of the games are, you know, obviously the microtransaction-y games.

48
00:04:40,510 --> 00:04:48,650
Rym: I also don't think any games make significantly more sales by being at conventions, except in very limited circumstances.

49
00:04:49,530 --> 00:04:55,330
Scott: The Nintendo's, the Sony's, the Microsoft's, they've, basically, E3 is completely dead, they've abandoned that.

50
00:04:55,650 --> 00:04:56,552
Rym: I mean, what human being?

51
00:04:56,752 --> 00:05:04,729
Rym: Who on earth wasn't going to buy Tears of the Kingdom, but then they saw it out of PAX and were like, "Oh shit, I should buy that Zelda

52
00:05:04,769 --> 00:05:04,970
Rym: game.".

53
00:05:05,170 --> 00:05:23,849
Scott: Right, but they've abandoned the E3, they've all realized, even Apple, that you can just announce your products with videos, which they've been doing, just Nintendo Directs, and then they're like, "Oh, you don't actually need to hold an in-person press event and take questions from the press and deal with annoying shit like

54
00:05:23,889 --> 00:05:23,970
Rym: that.".

55
00:05:24,971 --> 00:05:31,570
Scott: It's a lot cheaper and just as effective to put out a video that the media can watch at home and then write about.

56
00:05:32,111 --> 00:05:42,230
Scott: You're going to get the same press coverage with the video, it's cheaper, it's just as effective, and they're never going to go back to doing in-person, big, extravaganza-type events.

57
00:05:42,830 --> 00:05:57,210
Rym: Now, meanwhile, contrast this with MagFest, which is not just games, but also culture and art and everything else, and also has still the extremely strong culture of the only people who go to MagFest for a day are the people who couldn't go to MagFest for the whole time.

58
00:05:57,391 --> 00:06:02,789
Rym: That con is built around people who are all in for a weekend, like old faxes.

59
00:06:03,650 --> 00:06:11,090
Scott: Right, so if you look at what's going on at Pax, the things that people are doing is shopping, obviously.

60
00:06:11,350 --> 00:06:19,230
Scott: There's some amount of shopping, even though there's not that many people who sell things, and most of the ones who do are just selling tabletop stuff or vintage games.

61
00:06:20,571 --> 00:06:26,204
Scott: But those are things that people can't buy easily, perhaps, depending where they live, what stores are available.

62
00:06:26,485 --> 00:06:28,410
Scott: There might be unique stuff for them.

63
00:06:29,010 --> 00:06:30,333
Rym: I'll think about the old anime cons.

64
00:06:30,353 --> 00:06:32,999
Rym: There was a time when part of the appeal of an anime con was.

65
00:06:33,019 --> 00:06:37,770
Rym: there are things for sale there that are not for sale anywhere else because the internet doesn't really exist yet.

66
00:06:38,771 --> 00:06:42,616
Scott: And then the other thing is that, you know, amusements, right?

67
00:06:42,696 --> 00:06:52,149
Scott: So some of the booths, actually two of the biggest booths were Dunkin' Donuts and Mountain Dew, which shows you where it's going.

68
00:06:52,670 --> 00:06:56,510
Scott: They gotta sell this booth space somehow to make money to pay for the con.

69
00:06:56,810 --> 00:07:11,268
Rym: And a big company that's not a game company that has a big budget could look and say, "Oh, it's very expensive for us to put an ad on the side of a hockey arena of a good team, but it's really cheap to put it at that gaming thing, and they said 100,000 people go to it.

70
00:07:11,549 --> 00:07:12,130
Scott: Alright.".

71
00:07:12,630 --> 00:07:23,086
Scott: But what happened at those booths, the Dunkin' Donuts booth specifically, right, is they had some sort of game set up in the booth where you would have to touch this giant video wall.

72
00:07:23,106 --> 00:07:25,350
Scott: It was just basically a phone game, but big.

73
00:07:26,611 --> 00:07:33,781
Scott: But you waited in line, you played the game, and you could get stuff, like merch with Dunkin' logos on it and, you know, whatever.

74
00:07:34,222 --> 00:07:36,526
Scott: So you get $5 Dunkin' gift cards.

75
00:07:36,586 --> 00:07:39,150
Scott: I saw a couple people post, you know, those people who were...

76
00:07:39,752 --> 00:07:41,201
Rym: I hate everything you're saying.

77
00:07:41,281 --> 00:07:42,630
Rym: Every word just makes me mad.

78
00:07:42,830 --> 00:07:48,610
Scott: Yeah, the people who spend all their time in the expo hall, they had, like, enough Dunkin' gift cards to almost pay for their badge, perhaps.

79
00:07:49,390 --> 00:07:57,670
Scott: And, well, I guess if you considered, it's basically, if you think about it, it's buy enough coffee and donuts, get a PAX ticket free.

80
00:07:57,856 --> 00:07:58,005
Scott: Yep.

81
00:07:58,490 --> 00:08:03,559
Scott: Well, if you think of it backwards, but then you have to spend enough of your PAX time to get the...

82
00:08:10,451 --> 00:08:22,910
Rym: The reality is, the people who do that are just trying to get whatever reward, whatever serotonin button is at the end of PAX XP, or they're trying to get something worth dollars because they're poor and the economy's fucked.

83
00:08:23,631 --> 00:08:29,440
Scott: The only thing that the Marvel snap reward, you didn't have to actually do PAX XP.

84
00:08:29,520 --> 00:08:31,223
Scott: It's just the one at the front of the con.

85
00:08:31,243 --> 00:08:33,447
Scott: You go beep one time, there was no line.

86
00:08:33,467 --> 00:08:35,450
Scott: You just go beep one time and you get it.

87
00:08:35,650 --> 00:08:40,610
Scott: They don't actually have to go around and do the whole PAX XP to get the thingy, right?

88
00:08:42,371 --> 00:08:45,419
Scott: And the other thing is that all you have to do is find a QR code and beep it.

89
00:08:45,579 --> 00:08:49,410
Scott: You don't actually have to, like, actually engage, right?

90
00:08:50,731 --> 00:08:57,269
Scott: It's like, they should level up PAX XP to where it's like, no, bro, you gotta check something out in tabletop and play a fucking board game.

91
00:08:57,290 --> 00:08:57,450
Scott: For real.

92
00:08:57,470 --> 00:09:04,530
Rym: See, the thing is, I don't know if people would do that because my experience for being many years, like the enforcer role I usually have, is main theater.

93
00:09:05,030 --> 00:09:13,746
Rym: So some of the nights, if there's something in the theater I don't care that much about, I'll stand at the front and just be a guy, like, engaging attendees, or come by and like, "Hey, what's in the main theater?

94
00:09:13,986 --> 00:09:14,467
Rym: I'm lost.

95
00:09:14,507 --> 00:09:15,349
Rym: Where's the Albatross

96
00:09:15,369 --> 00:09:15,649
Rym: Theater?"

97
00:09:15,669 --> 00:09:15,990
Rym: or whatever.

98
00:09:16,611 --> 00:09:29,770
Rym: But I would say 60% of the people who walk by at night are looking for PAX XP and, in my personal opinion, give zero fucks about fucking anything except completing the PAX XP because there's some reward they're going to get.

99
00:09:30,050 --> 00:09:39,130
Rym: They're, like, so hyper-focused that I even have a sign I just hold that I pop up that just says the PAX XP thing is not up here because I just don't want to deal with them.

100
00:09:40,751 --> 00:09:44,418
Scott: But, yeah, I don't think you get, I don't know what you actually get if you do more.

101
00:09:44,499 --> 00:09:49,590
Scott: It's like, you could do just the one to get the most valuable reward and it's 100% guaranteed you're going to get it.

102
00:09:50,070 --> 00:09:52,074
Scott: So I don't know what you get by doing them all.

103
00:09:52,154 --> 00:09:59,450
Scott: I think there's, like, a chance to win a gaming computer, but it's, like, I don't know if doing more of them gets you more chances or not.

104
00:10:00,030 --> 00:10:05,106
Rym: I'm just increasingly, like, these structures are toxic in society.

105
00:10:05,507 --> 00:10:06,510
Rym: Everywhere that they exist.

106
00:10:07,312 --> 00:10:13,925
Scott: Basically, it's, like, people just being manipulated by giving them things that don't really exist, right?

107
00:10:14,446 --> 00:10:16,210
Scott: Giving people JPEGs, right?

108
00:10:17,451 --> 00:10:24,270
Scott: You know, we need to somehow inoculate human beings from being influenced by giving them nothing of value.

109
00:10:24,510 --> 00:10:28,719
Scott: It's, like, you give me something of value and I will give you something of no value.

110
00:10:28,779 --> 00:10:33,650
Scott: that somehow makes you feel like you have received value when you have, in fact, received nothing.

111
00:10:33,851 --> 00:10:36,644
Rym: Scott, is that not just religion?

112
00:10:36,905 --> 00:10:37,850
Rym: This is an uphill battle.

113
00:10:39,211 --> 00:10:40,059
Scott: At least, you know what?

114
00:10:40,119 --> 00:10:41,350
Scott: I gotta say, right?

115
00:10:41,570 --> 00:10:45,349
Rym: At least the religion gave you some wine, a little bit of bread, sometimes a song.

116
00:10:45,850 --> 00:10:51,610
Scott: If you go to some church or a synagogue or a mosque or whatever, a Quaker meeting thingy.

117
00:10:51,750 --> 00:10:53,990
Scott: It's like getting an hour and a half play happens.

118
00:10:54,770 --> 00:11:06,270
Scott: It's, like, you're gonna, there is going to be, whether you believe in it or not, invest in it or not, build your life around it or not, you're gonna hear public speaking, you're gonna hear music, right?

119
00:11:06,350 --> 00:11:09,530
Scott: Maybe some organ action, maybe, depending on what it is, singing.

120
00:11:10,857 --> 00:11:12,070
Rym: Someone might bring out a snake.

121
00:11:12,990 --> 00:11:22,210
Scott: Yeah, it's not really too different than going to a concert or a lecture or both at the same time or who knows what else that religion might do.

122
00:11:22,330 --> 00:11:28,029
Scott: Dancing, all kinds of things, food, things are gonna happen there that are of value.

123
00:11:29,214 --> 00:11:33,030
Scott: Regardless of the belief in, you know, things that don't exist or not.

124
00:11:35,192 --> 00:11:41,310
Rym: But these, like, I feel like part of what's going on is the economic situation.

125
00:11:41,650 --> 00:11:50,190
Rym: Like, people gravitate toward things that they perceive as free or things where they can trade anything other than dollars to get anything.

126
00:11:51,132 --> 00:11:52,909
Scott: People collected baseball cards before.

127
00:11:53,511 --> 00:12:06,310
Rym: True, but even then, a lot of people collected baseball cards partly because of their perceived rarity, which was then validated by some of them were rare, which was then ruined by then X-Men made everything foil and tricked people into buying, like, Cyclops' wedding.

128
00:12:06,430 --> 00:12:14,110
Scott: Anyway, we'll see what happens to PAX because all the other conventions, the ones that are the not-profit conventions, right, seem to be exploding.

129
00:12:14,270 --> 00:12:16,550
Scott: The MAGFest exploded, the anime Boston exploded.

130
00:12:16,550 --> 00:12:27,630
Rym: Well, because MAGFest is a cultural phenomenon and the problem is early PAXes before the single day badges were a lot more like MAGFest in terms of how the attendees engaged with each other and the con.

131
00:12:28,310 --> 00:12:35,390
Rym: And that, like, that really starkly shifted as soon as they started selling single day badges, especially at PAX East.

132
00:12:35,791 --> 00:12:46,689
Scott: But right now, right, I think what the MAGFest has, the anime Boston has, that the PAX doesn't have as much of anymore is amusement.

133
00:12:47,710 --> 00:12:54,690
Scott: Going to MAGFest is closer to going to an amusement park, right, where the concerts don't stop, right?

134
00:12:54,970 --> 00:12:57,021
Scott: The arcade is always open.

135
00:12:57,383 --> 00:12:58,610
Scott: There's the museum.

136
00:12:58,730 --> 00:13:06,590
Rym: But also, there are people engaging socially with strangers fucking everywhere at MAGFest.

137
00:13:06,790 --> 00:13:12,386
Rym: You cannot walk without, if you want to talk to people or see a cosplayer do something weird, less so.

138
00:13:14,150 --> 00:13:19,810
Scott: The point is, is that the con itself is, you know, the MIVs area, right?

139
00:13:19,950 --> 00:13:22,550
Scott: It's like, it is an amusement field, right?

140
00:13:22,891 --> 00:13:27,830
Scott: It's like, even if you're there completely alone, the con is entertaining the crowd.

141
00:13:27,910 --> 00:13:34,850
Scott: Whereas at PAX, right, the only things that are entertaining the crowd are the, is the programming by the enforcers.

142
00:13:35,010 --> 00:13:39,264
Scott: The expo hall isn't, is mostly not entertainment for the crowd.

143
00:13:39,284 --> 00:13:41,010
Scott: It is mostly advertisements.

144
00:13:41,850 --> 00:13:45,650
Scott: You know, if you had ad blog for PAX, the expo hall would just be invisible, right?

145
00:13:47,115 --> 00:13:55,390
Scott: Other than, like, you know, some small indie people doing demos, those are still ads technically, but they're also amusements because they're demos of games you never would have seen anywhere else.

146
00:13:55,430 --> 00:13:56,635
Rym: Now, conversely, look at PAX Unplugged.

147
00:13:56,675 --> 00:14:16,850
Rym: because of the tabletop focus, and partly because tabletop people are pretty hardcore, they see that, that is, PAX has provided a space and a library to play tabletop games for people who often do not have a good opportunity to do so in their day-to-day lives, and that's why PAX Unplugged was brimming to absolute chaotic capacity.

148
00:14:17,590 --> 00:14:32,470
Scott: Right, but PAX's, you know, zones of amusement, right, are, two of them are console freeplay and PC freeplay, which, you know, unless you're entering tournaments, I saw a Vampire Survivors tournament, that was awesome.

149
00:14:34,851 --> 00:14:39,890
Scott: Unless you're entering tournaments, there's nothing there that you can't just do at home, right?

150
00:14:40,492 --> 00:14:45,290
Scott: It's not really, it's like, why am I paying to go to PAX to sit at a Playstation?

151
00:14:45,590 --> 00:14:45,972
Scott: Well, partly

152
00:14:46,012 --> 00:15:17,550
Rym: because older, older PAX's, and again, this is the before they had the single day badges, because the people would be there for the whole weekend, and because it was kind of the same people, mostly, like there was this core that came to every PAX, you'd sort of like, you'd interact with strangers who are your PAX friends, and thus, the experience does not play Smash Brothers, or play like, I don't know, the cool Bomberman, the experience is, I'm in Seattle, or I'm in Boston, and I'm playing cool Bomberman with these people that I only see every year at this convention.

153
00:15:18,070 --> 00:15:20,337
Scott: Look at the Magfest console area, right?

154
00:15:20,437 --> 00:15:23,145
Scott: It still is mostly just game consoles.

155
00:15:23,185 --> 00:15:24,810
Scott: you could just play at home, right?

156
00:15:25,170 --> 00:15:27,838
Scott: But, they got that challenge area, right?

157
00:15:28,219 --> 00:15:31,408
Scott: They got games up on those big screens.

158
00:15:31,468 --> 00:15:35,450
Scott: they set up, with like, you know, the quads for their tournaments and things like that, right?

159
00:15:35,870 --> 00:15:46,789
Scott: So, it's like, there's something about it, right, that is elevated in experience, right, versus, you know, the way PAX has it.

160
00:15:48,171 --> 00:16:12,310
Scott: And so, it's like, you know, there is an amusement park-ish, you know, attraction to the entertainment that is there, and I think if PAX wants to bring more people in, you know, I think that that's what they should do, is dedicate more space and resources to providing, you know, unique, entertaining things for the attendees, right?

161
00:16:12,890 --> 00:16:18,030
Rym: Hard mode, those things cost resources to produce, but do not generate resources.

162
00:16:18,030 --> 00:16:23,228
Scott: Yeah, right, and if you're for, that's a problem when you're for profit, but when you're not for profit, well, that's all you do, right?

163
00:16:23,650 --> 00:16:45,805
Rym: And also, I think the problem is, I think the divide between seeking the people who wouldn't otherwise come to PAX, but come for a day because of the expo hall and the ads and the other things, I think those people are subsidizing the rest of PAX, but at the same time, if we dropped a lot of that crap, PAX would be smaller, it'd be a lot more like PAX was in like 2009, 2010 out in Seattle.

164
00:16:48,090 --> 00:16:58,890
Scott: I mean, when you're making money selling ads, whatever form those take, you have to put some effort into the ad selling, but you also got to put effort into increasing your eyeballs, right?

165
00:16:59,270 --> 00:17:09,310
Scott: So I think, you know, PAX needs to do more investment on the increasing eyeballs area, which will translate into making their ads more valuable.

166
00:17:09,589 --> 00:17:30,670
Rym: PAX East is a hard mode because there aren't enough hotels, there's not like, there's a lot of factors that make PAX East the single day attendee PAX, whereas West has less of that problem, has more of the original PAX spirit still, and has more amusements going on, but that's partly because it's warm and it's enmeshed in Seattle in what I think might be the best convention center in America now.

167
00:17:31,453 --> 00:17:32,428
Scott: I don't know about that.

168
00:17:32,971 --> 00:17:34,109
Scott: I haven't been to a better convention center.

169
00:17:34,150 --> 00:17:35,389
