198
00:20:12,690 --> 00:20:17,730
Rym: But Caps, won 36 games, lost 27 games, got 10 points from making it to OT.

199
00:20:18,691 --> 00:20:21,589
Scott: Boston has 15 points from making it to OT.

200
00:20:21,991 --> 00:20:24,985
Rym: Yup, Rangers have 4 points from making it to OT.

201
00:20:25,025 --> 00:20:25,990
Rym: Rangers won their games.

202
00:20:26,650 --> 00:20:36,950
Scott: That's right, so it's like, you look at Boston, Boston only has, like, as many wins as Toronto, a team 2 spaces behind them, right?

203
00:20:38,031 --> 00:20:45,510
Scott: And Florida, who has 4 more wins than Boston, is behind Boston because of these OT, you know, loser points, right?

204
00:20:47,592 --> 00:20:53,885
Scott: And so, yeah, you know, standings don't directly correlate to quality because the point system is messed up.

205
00:20:56,410 --> 00:21:01,590
Rym: Yup, I'm just excited that the Rangers are clinched and we'll definitely go to some playoff games and we'll see how this goes.

206
00:21:01,870 --> 00:21:07,661
Scott: You're guaranteed to go to 1.

207
00:21:07,661 --> 00:21:08,309
Rym: You got any news?

208
00:21:09,371 --> 00:21:23,469
Scott: I don't know, it's not really news, but at PAX East, I walked around the expo hall, most of the stuff was stuff I'd seen before, or was stuff that wasn't a game, or was stuff that wasn't worth spending my time on, I knew what it was, like, do I need to go to the Final Fantasy MMO booth?

209
00:21:23,690 --> 00:21:23,850
Scott: No.

210
00:21:25,431 --> 00:21:38,750
Scott: But there was one game that I spent a few minutes hearing about, that is, as a game, just a really basic trivia game, not much going on in terms of the game rules or any of that action, right?

211
00:21:39,510 --> 00:21:50,350
Scott: But it was basically a trivia game, and the thing that makes a trivia game exciting is not the game part, but the content of the trivia, right?

212
00:21:50,710 --> 00:22:06,550
Scott: A family of people who were all lawyers, judges, involved in the US criminal law system made a game full of trivia questions about misdemeanors and felonies in US law in general.

213
00:22:06,730 --> 00:22:18,750
Scott: Obviously you can have some variances by state and whatnot, but overall, the things in this game are true of the legal system in the United States, of US law, as of now-ish.

214
00:22:19,451 --> 00:22:21,835
Scott: The game is called Crime No Crime.

215
00:22:22,135 --> 00:22:34,883
Rym: It already reminds me a lot of an ancient, ancient PC game I used to play called Jurisdiction, and all it was is you'd see a short court scene, and you could decide whether or not, as the lawyer for the defendant, you should object.

216
00:22:34,904 --> 00:22:36,530
Rym: That's it.

217
00:22:37,450 --> 00:22:41,940
Scott: Well this is basically, such and such happened, is that a crime?

218
00:22:42,782 --> 00:22:43,745
Scott: What crime is it?

219
00:22:43,965 --> 00:22:45,990
Scott: Who's guilty of the crime, right?

220
00:22:46,030 --> 00:22:53,370
Rym: I feel like if you have a naively positive view of the United States, your default should be to just say everything you don't think is a crime is yes, because you'll probably be right.

221
00:22:53,790 --> 00:22:57,710
Rym: But simultaneously, if you are a cynical person, you can play it pretty seriously.

222
00:22:58,190 --> 00:23:18,930
Scott: Basically every single question in the game, and they have a huge box full of questions, is basically, you think about it, and you're like, hmm, well, I am a person of basic moral judgement, and the vast majority of people, including you, say, that person, you know, did this, right?

223
00:23:19,350 --> 00:23:24,830
Scott: And that person did that, so that person should be in this much trouble, that person should be in no trouble, right?

224
00:23:24,970 --> 00:23:26,674
Scott: There'll be some variance between people.

225
00:23:27,156 --> 00:23:32,690
Scott: And then the real answer is, nope, US law is bullshit, that person is totally fucked even though they barely did anything.

226
00:23:34,051 --> 00:23:40,830
Scott: And that's basically the answer to every question, is opposite of common sense every time, because the US law is bullshit.

227
00:23:42,515 --> 00:23:46,329
Scott: And it's like, you know that already, you don't need a game to tell you that, you knew.

228
00:23:47,010 --> 00:23:58,650
Scott: But it's a strong reminder, and it helps you learn specific scenarios and situations, right, that you may not know, you know, the specific ones, even though you know that in general is bullshit.

229
00:23:59,512 --> 00:24:09,810
Scott: And it can be very educational, especially for young people, people in places where there's a lot of crime, people who aren't white, who the cops will harass more frequently, right?

230
00:24:10,270 --> 00:24:18,850
Scott: Of, you know, what is and what is not a crime, and how much of a crime something is, how much trouble you'll get in for doing a certain thing, right?

231
00:24:20,752 --> 00:24:23,710
Scott: It'll make you angry, I guess, to play this game, but also enlightened.

232
00:24:24,691 --> 00:24:29,270
Rym: Yep, unless you're a law nerd, in which case you'll just sigh every single question.

233
00:24:30,013 --> 00:24:31,589
Scott: Yeah, you'll know all of them, you'll be like, yep.

234
00:24:31,830 --> 00:24:34,289
Rym: But you'll sigh because you'll be like, yep, that's a bullshit law.

235
00:24:34,430 --> 00:24:38,067
Scott: You'll probably try to come up with new questions, like, oh, you should put a question about this one in the game.

236
00:24:38,147 --> 00:24:38,630
Scott: Oh, it's in there.

237
00:24:39,920 --> 00:24:40,769
Scott: Yeah, we got that one.

238
00:24:41,630 --> 00:24:50,090
Scott: So, I guess if you're not in the United States, I recommend this game for learning about how bullshit the US is.

239
00:24:50,592 --> 00:24:51,784
Scott: Of course, you already know that, too.

240
00:24:52,551 --> 00:24:56,610
Rym: Yeah, it's not like, we don't talk about that on GeekNights that often, do we?

241
00:24:57,690 --> 00:24:57,862
Scott: Yeah.

242
00:24:59,811 --> 00:25:02,819
Scott: But that was, I was very, you know, it was one of the better things, I think.

243
00:25:02,879 --> 00:25:05,126
Scott: It was just a little booth in, like, the far back corner.

244
00:25:05,166 --> 00:25:06,530
Scott: They clearly was a cheapy booth.

245
00:25:08,110 --> 00:25:17,830
Scott: But it's, you know, they had a really attractive pitch because they would just come up to you, they had a bunch of people, and they would go up to you and they'd just start asking you questions, like, hey, if so-and-so did this, is that a crime?

246
00:25:18,431 --> 00:25:22,720
Scott: And you'd be like, you'd be like, you start standing there, you're like, hmm, and you start talking to them, right?

247
00:25:22,780 --> 00:25:26,748
Scott: Like, yeah, I know US law is bullshit, that's probably a fucking crime, isn't it?

248
00:25:26,768 --> 00:25:27,730
Scott: They're like, yes, sure is.

249
00:25:34,222 --> 00:25:35,580
Rym: Things of the day!

250
00:25:36,560 --> 00:25:41,795
Rym: So, Joe Haver does fantastic animations and skits, like, I really like his comedy this year.

251
00:25:41,815 --> 00:25:43,560
Rym: You know, I linked to him once or twice in the past.

252
00:25:44,880 --> 00:25:52,013
Rym: But this year he's doing something different and doing, like, every month a longer form for real, like, different kind of format.

253
00:25:52,454 --> 00:25:55,640
Rym: But he's also got a separate channel where he talks about movies.

254
00:25:56,500 --> 00:26:11,549
Rym: And he just did an episode two days ago titled "When I Watch Something Unforgettable" and he talks about how he's not, like, a super anime fan, like, he doesn't watch a lot of TV, and while people around him That's like me, I don't watch TV.

255
00:26:11,610 --> 00:26:12,840
Scott: I just watch movies.

256
00:26:12,920 --> 00:26:18,260
Rym: People around him have been extremely affected by the end of Evangelion.

257
00:26:19,320 --> 00:26:21,254
Scott: Okay, but did they watch the TV show, though?

258
00:26:21,294 --> 00:26:22,020
Scott: You can't really watch that.

259
00:26:22,060 --> 00:26:22,601
Rym: Oh, no, they all had.

260
00:26:22,742 --> 00:26:30,620
Rym: So, Joe Haver decided, even though he's not really a TV person, nor does he watch much anime other than, like, the occasional anime movie.

261
00:26:31,460 --> 00:26:32,460
Scott: Even though he's an animator.

262
00:26:33,823 --> 00:26:36,389
Rym: But he just decides he's going to put in the effort.

263
00:26:36,670 --> 00:26:40,780
Rym: He watches all 26 episodes of Evangelion just to have the full context.

264
00:26:41,200 --> 00:26:48,275
Rym: Then he watched "End of Evangelion" to see, alright, is this top tier cinema?

265
00:26:48,455 --> 00:26:50,560
Rym: or are all my weeb friends fucking with me?

266
00:26:51,560 --> 00:26:51,984
Rym: Both.

267
00:26:52,467 --> 00:26:53,093
Rym: Both are true.

268
00:26:54,162 --> 00:26:54,859
Scott: It's a good video.

269
00:26:54,880 --> 00:26:55,647
Scott: To some extent.

270
00:26:56,474 --> 00:26:57,160
Scott: Also, neither.

271
00:26:57,802 --> 00:26:58,785
Rym: It's pretty good.

272
00:26:58,805 --> 00:27:04,300
Rym: I love seeing someone who is not deeply enmeshed in the culture around a certain kind of media.

273
00:27:04,860 --> 00:27:08,260
Rym: Who is otherwise literate in media to give their opinion on something.

274
00:27:08,820 --> 00:27:10,660
Scott: Like, Cicely and Eber talking about an anime movie was great.

275
00:27:10,680 --> 00:27:12,360
Scott: Did they watch it on Netflix where it's slightly incorrect?

276
00:27:13,300 --> 00:27:23,340
Rym: But, uh, Joe Haver, the gist of this 11 minute video is basically, wow, that movie and that show are fucking brilliant.

277
00:27:23,440 --> 00:27:29,060
Rym: I, like, had to go outside and walk around quietly for a while just to sort of mull it over.

278
00:27:29,861 --> 00:27:32,640
Rym: I don't think he even knows about the remake movies yet, so.

279
00:27:34,340 --> 00:27:41,520
Rym: But it was interesting to see a non-weeb have the same reaction that a lot of us have had at the end of, like, a Cowboy Bebop or an Evangelion.

280
00:27:41,580 --> 00:27:44,559
Rym: Where you kind of just sit there for a while going, hmm.

281
00:27:45,640 --> 00:27:49,979
Scott: Did you tell him that it aired on, you know, kids' afternoon programming right after Ninja Turtles?

282
00:27:53,284 --> 00:27:54,151
Rym: So you gotta think of the day?

283
00:27:54,171 --> 00:27:54,978
Rym: I assume you do.

284
00:27:55,960 --> 00:28:03,720
Scott: Yeah, so recently I was doing some research on the current state of over-the-air TV broadcasts in the US that still exist, kind of.

285
00:28:05,440 --> 00:28:16,880
Scott: And I was reading more about some pirate radio action, and I see here this article published on March 28th, just a few days ago, about original pirate material.

286
00:28:17,821 --> 00:28:23,660
Scott: And it's actually about pirate radio in Europe, which is something I didn't know about at all, really.

287
00:28:23,880 --> 00:28:31,640
Scott: But it makes a lot of sense, because the geography of Europe, right, in the US, if you want to do pirate radio, you gotta put your antenna on, like, land somewhere.

288
00:28:32,161 --> 00:28:34,719
Rym: And the FCC has jurisdiction over all of that land.

289
00:28:35,301 --> 00:28:38,980
Scott: That's right, and you're gonna get found out, probably, if you make a trouble.

290
00:28:39,742 --> 00:28:41,592
Scott: And, yeah, it's not so easy to do.

291
00:28:41,673 --> 00:28:42,940
Scott: People have done it, obviously.

292
00:28:44,142 --> 00:28:50,180
Scott: But in Europe, you know, a lot of places in Europe, say the UK, where it's just an island, it's not that big.

293
00:28:51,321 --> 00:28:55,580
Scott: You know, Denmark, it's like a Michigan of Europe kind of, right?

294
00:28:55,940 --> 00:28:59,060
Scott: It's like, you know, small countries, lot of water.

295
00:29:00,500 --> 00:29:11,340
Scott: People have gone out into international waters with boats, put up big ol' antennas on those boats, and just broadcasted pirate radio out into the mainland.

296
00:29:12,122 --> 00:29:15,720
Scott: And as international waters, no one can stop them easily, really.

297
00:29:16,562 --> 00:29:18,399
Scott: I guess they could go blow them up or something, I don't know.

298
00:29:19,885 --> 00:29:20,678
Scott: Is that a crime?

299
00:29:21,981 --> 00:29:22,732
Scott: I guess, sure.

300
00:29:25,583 --> 00:29:27,960
Scott: You know, but yeah, this is really fascinating, right?

301
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:33,299
Scott: The history of the people who actually did this, what they broadcast, how they did it, what happened, etc.

302
00:29:34,382 --> 00:29:36,054
Scott: Apparently it was big in, you know, the '70s.

303
00:29:37,382 --> 00:29:41,439
Scott: It's not so big now because people don't use the radio so much.

304
00:29:42,941 --> 00:29:49,280
Scott: They even have a picture of a small portable AM/FM radio with, like, the really shitty one-ear headphone, you know, those old ones.

305
00:29:50,741 --> 00:30:03,420
Scott: From 1964, the pirate radio station was making branded radios to sell to people so that they could listen to their, you know, radio station that was out in the water from anywhere that it could reach.

306
00:30:04,801 --> 00:30:12,460
Rym: I'm just, I am such a fan of pirate radio specifically as a concept.

307
00:30:12,700 --> 00:30:24,320
Rym: Because it flies in the face of a lot of the way the world is structured, in a way that can't hurt people too directly, and is like the maximum amount of fun mischief.

308
00:30:24,800 --> 00:30:32,520
Rym: That you could, I mean, you can send photons in every direction, at scale, and other people can hear things that are illegal in a place that they are.

309
00:30:32,882 --> 00:30:33,023
Scott: Right.

310
00:30:33,325 --> 00:30:35,600
Scott: Well, GeekNights is not illegal, right?

311
00:30:35,760 --> 00:30:42,560
Scott: But I think of it as basically pirate radio because what you have is you have these official channels, right, platforms.

312
00:30:42,820 --> 00:30:45,639
Scott: The Facebooks, the Twitters, and whatnot, right?

313
00:30:46,220 --> 00:30:52,780
Scott: The TikToks, the YouTubes that are like the official channels of, you know, legitimacy in society.

314
00:30:53,423 --> 00:30:56,580
Scott: And basically every other website is pirate radio, right?

315
00:30:56,680 --> 00:31:04,400
Scott: It's just somebody raised on their own with no, you know, official designation or permission.

316
00:31:04,720 --> 00:31:12,500
Rym: I rode my boat out into GeoCities and I made a site and all it did was host a handful of ROMs of translated Japanese versions of Final Fantasy.

317
00:31:13,180 --> 00:31:19,040
Scott: Just brought anyone just on their own deciding what to broadcast with no permission from anybody, right?

318
00:31:19,662 --> 00:31:21,300
Scott: Just publishing what you want to publish.

319
00:31:21,660 --> 00:31:23,720
Scott: You know, zines, zines are pirate radio, right?

320
00:31:23,760 --> 00:31:27,460
Scott: You're not in a real magazine that has like a publisher and whatever making money.

321
00:31:27,962 --> 00:31:31,420
Scott: You just went and printed them and started giving them out yourself, right?

322
00:31:31,983 --> 00:31:33,159
Scott: All that stuff is pirate radio.

323
00:31:34,021 --> 00:31:39,440
Scott: And my goal is to basically one day, all the media I consume should be of that variety.

324
00:31:40,823 --> 00:31:42,399
Scott: Just avoid anything else.

325
00:31:43,043 --> 00:31:45,640
Rym: In the meta moment, the GeekNights Book Club book, Walk on Girl.

326
00:31:45,820 --> 00:31:46,859
Rym: The night is short.

327
00:31:47,080 --> 00:31:51,840
Rym: I expect we'll do the show, give you guys like three-ish weeks.

328
00:31:52,185 --> 00:31:53,180
Rym: That's not a long book.

329
00:31:53,960 --> 00:31:55,200
Scott: I'm going to finish reading it soon.

330
00:31:55,761 --> 00:31:58,720
Scott: I made significant progress on the way to From PAX.

331
00:31:59,060 --> 00:32:04,412
Rym: Yep, I got some travel time because I got to... We're heading out to Rochester to watch the Total Eclipse of the Sun.

332
00:32:04,835 --> 00:32:05,940
Scott: that's happening next week.

333
00:32:06,320 --> 00:32:06,573
Scott: Safely.

334
00:32:07,421 --> 00:32:11,600
Rym: Yeah, so total aside, if any of... This is a public service announcement.

335
00:32:11,940 --> 00:32:14,078
Scott: Either wear a welding mask or use the box.

336
00:32:14,340 --> 00:32:18,060
Rym: Naughty, you have to wear a specific kind of welding mask or you're still fucked.

337
00:32:18,140 --> 00:32:18,840
Rym: But here's the problem.

338
00:32:19,201 --> 00:32:23,659
Rym: There's an article on Waypo and they made it public and there's a lot of news talking about this.

339
00:32:24,521 --> 00:32:37,760
Rym: Unlike even the last Total Solar Eclipse, the number of super illegal, just fake eclipse glasses out there might be more than the legitimate ones and it's very hard to tell if you're not an expert.

340
00:32:39,061 --> 00:32:50,520
Rym: If you bought your eclipse glasses basically from any website that wasn't one of a very small list of them, I'm putting Amazon on the list of do not buy, you might literally blind yourself.

